5. Hardware Meritocracy (PoHM)

Proof of Hardware Meritocracy (PoHM) is Pacyte's foundational innovation in consensus design. It replaces the capital-centric selection of traditional PoS with a dual-barrier system: verifiable hardware capability plus economic stake.

5.1 The CPUID Gatekeeper

To register as a Titan validator, a node must pass a hardware verification check using the x86_64 CPUID instruction. This is not a simulation, benchmark, or software-based test—it directly queries the physical processor's feature registers.

Required Instruction Sets:

· AVX-512F (Foundation)
· AVX-512BW (Byte and Word)
· AVX-512DQ (Doubleword and Quadword)
· AVX-512VL (Vector Length Extensions)

Optional Bonus Multipliers: AVX-512VNNI, AVX-512IFMA, AVX-512VBMI, AVX-512VAES

On ARM64 architectures (Apple Silicon, AWS Graviton), the CPUID check returns false, ensuring Titans operate exclusively on enterprise-grade x86_64 server hardware. This architectural requirement is fundamental to the protocol's performance guarantees.

5.2 Continuous ZK-Proof Benchmarking

Hardware capability is not a one-time check. Titans are continuously tested on Zero-Knowledge proof generation speed using a standardized Groth16 prover circuit for a pre-defined statement.

ZK-Proof Latency Merit Score Impact Fee Multiplier Consequence
Under 500ms × 1.02 (Bonus tier) Bonus rewards Active, bonus eligible
500–800ms × 1.0 (Standard tier) Standard rewards Active, standard
Above 800ms × 0.95 (Penalty tier) Reduced rewards Active, penalized; Consecutive Fails +1
10 Consecutive Fails — — Automatic ejection from validator set

The Merit Score is a floating-point value ranging from 0 to 200, initialized at 100 for newly registered Titans. Validator rewards are proportional to merit_score × √(stake_amount), creating a dual incentive structure where both high-performance hardware and significant economic commitment contribute to reward maximization.

