6. Hybrid Post-Quantum Cryptography

Pacyte Nexus implements a defense-in-depth cryptographic architecture that provides security against both classical and quantum adversaries. Rather than replacing classical cryptography entirely—which would sacrifice performance and compatibility—Pacyte requires both a classical and a post-quantum signature for every transaction and consensus vote.

6.1 Dual Signature Scheme

Layer Algorithm Public Key Size Signature Size Security Level Standard
Classical Ed25519 32 bytes 64 bytes 128-bit classical IETF RFC 8032
Post-Quantum Dilithium5 2,592 bytes ~4,595 bytes NIST Security Level 5 (AES-256 equivalent) NIST FIPS 204

Dilithium5 is a lattice-based signature scheme selected by NIST in 2024 as the primary post-quantum signature standard. NIST Security Level 5 means breaking it is as computationally difficult as brute-forcing a 256-bit AES key—the highest security level defined by NIST for post-quantum cryptography.

Why both signatures? Ed25519 provides fast, compact signatures with minimal blockchain bloat for current operations. Dilithium5 ensures that even if Shor's Algorithm running on a sufficiently powerful quantum computer completely breaks Ed25519, the Pacyte chain's history remains immutable and future transactions remain secure. No other major Layer-1 blockchain currently offers this dual protection from genesis.

6.2 Signature Verification Dispatch

Verification dispatches automatically by signature length:

· 64 bytes → Ed25519 verification path (standard elliptic curve)
· ~4,595 bytes → Dilithium5 verification path (post-quantum lattice)
· All other lengths → Rejected as invalid

This dispatch mechanism enables efficient verification without requiring additional metadata fields. The HybridSigner struct encapsulates both signing keys and produces the combined signature format.

6.3 Long-Term Security Guarantee

In the event that classical elliptic curve cryptography is broken by quantum adversaries, the Pacyte chain maintains forward secrecy through Dilithium5. Past transactions signed with Ed25519 remain secure because the independently valid Dilithium5 signature path is quantum-resistant. This property—forward secrecy against quantum adversaries—is unique among current Layer-1 protocols.

6.4 HSM Compatibility

The validator signing architecture is designed to support Hardware Security Modules (HSMs) such as YubiHSM2 and Ledger Vault. In production, validator private keys will never leave the secure hardware element. The key management layer abstracts signing operations behind a trait, enabling HSM integration without protocol changes.

6.5 Triple-Hash Engine

Pacyte employs a unique three-algorithm hash architecture, deploying each algorithm where it performs optimally:

Algorithm Primary Use Case Key Properties
SHA-256 Block hashing, signature pre-hashing, PoHM benchmarks Bitcoin standard, FIPS 180-4, 15+ years of continuous cryptanalytic resistance
Keccak-256 EVM compatibility, smart contract address generation, state trie operations Ethereum standard, NIST FIPS 202 (SHA-3), sponge construction, resistant to length extension attacks
BLAKE3 Internal operations, WASM execution, AVX-512 accelerated processing 10-15× faster than SHA-256 on AVX-512 hardware, Rust-native implementation, inherently parallelizable

