7. Consensus Engine: HotStuff BFT

Pacyte utilizes the HotStuff Byzantine Fault Tolerant protocol, the same consensus algorithm powering Aptos and Sui. It provides linear communication complexity O(N), optimistic responsiveness, and immediate finality without the probabilistic confirmation delays of Nakamoto consensus.

7.1 Round Lifecycle

```
NewRound → Proposing → Prevoting → Precommitting → Committed
     ↑                                                    |
     └────────────────── (next height) ←──────────────────┘
```

Phase Details:

1. NewRound: The validator set selects a proposer for the current height and round via deterministic round-robin.
2. Proposing: The designated proposer creates a block from the mempool, signs it with a hybrid signature, and broadcasts a Proposal message.
3. Prevoting: All validators validate the proposal and broadcast a Prevote for the accepted block hash.
4. Precommitting: Upon reaching Prevote Quorum (15+ signatures for the same block hash), validators broadcast a Precommit.
5. Committed: Upon Precommit Quorum, the block is irreversibly committed and the height increments.

7.2 Timeout Handling

If any phase times out, the Pacemaker module advances to the next round with exponential backoff:

· base_timeout × 1.5^(consecutive_timeouts)
· Capped at base_timeout × 10
· Maximum 10 rounds per height before error

7.3 Safety and Liveness Guarantees

HotStuff BFT provides accountable safety —even if up to one-third of validators (7 out of 21) are malicious, honest validators can produce cryptographic quorum certificates proving which blocks were committed, preventing long-range forks. The protocol guarantees optimistic responsiveness , meaning that during periods of network synchrony with an honest leader, blocks are committed at network speed without waiting for maximum timeouts.

