8. The Sentinel Watcher Layer

The Sentinel Layer introduces a formal separation of powers between block production and network auditing—a principle well-established in constitutional governance but absent from blockchain architecture until now.

8.1 Concept

In existing networks, validators both produce blocks and effectively self-audit, creating an inherent conflict of interest. Pacyte separates these functions:

Role Hardware Required Primary Function Economic Incentive
Titan (Validator) AVX-512, 32+ physical cores Block production, consensus voting, transaction ordering Transaction fee allocation
Sentinel (Watcher) Standard consumer hardware (any modern laptop/desktop) Real-time validator monitoring, anomaly detection, slashing evidence generation Network integrity (public good); future slashing reward sharing

8.2 Operational Mechanics

A Pacyte node started without the --validator flag automatically enters Sentinel mode. No additional configuration, stake, or registration is required.

The Sentinel:

1. Subscribes to the P2P network's gossip protocol.
2. Listens for all Proposal messages (new blocks proposed by Titans).
3. Listens for all Vote messages (Prevote and Precommit from Titans).
4. Maintains a structured SentinelReport for each observed height.
5. Logs all anomalies via the tracing subsystem.
6. Persists state to disk for crash recovery.

8.3 State Machine

```
Idle → Watching → Reporting → Idle
         ↑            |
         └─── Error ──┘
```

· Idle: Sentinel has not yet been activated.
· Watching: Actively monitoring network traffic and processing Proposal/Vote messages.
· Reporting: Generating a detailed summary report (triggered every report_interval blocks, default 10).
· Error: Network timeout or internal processing failure; automatically retries based on max_retries configuration.

8.4 Anomaly Detection Capabilities

The Sentinel detects 11 distinct anomaly types , each with a structured data payload for programmatic processing:

AnomalyType Description Severity Slashing Eligible
DoubleProposal Validator proposed two different blocks at the same height and round Critical ✅ Yes
DoubleVote Validator voted for two different block hashes at the same height and round Critical ✅ Yes
InvalidTimestamp Block timestamp differs from network median by >30 seconds Warning No
MissedBlock Validator failed to produce a block when scheduled as leader Moderate No (triggers ejection at 50+)
LowQuorum Fewer than 15 validators participated in consensus Critical No (indicates network partition)
GenesisViolation A block was proposed at height 0 with round > 0 Critical No
NonSequentialHeight Block height is not sequential with the previous observed height Warning No
InvalidSignature A validator's signature failed cryptographic verification Critical No (transaction rejected)
ProposalWithoutVote A block was proposed but received zero votes Warning No
VoteWithoutProposal A vote was observed without a corresponding proposal Warning No
StakeBelowThreshold A validator's staked amount fell below the minimum requirement Moderate No (triggers deactivation)

8.5 Automated Slashing Evidence Generation

When a Sentinel detects a DoubleProposal or DoubleVote anomaly, it automatically generates a SlashingEvidence struct containing:

· Validator identifier
· Block height and round
· Both conflicting block hashes
· Timestamp of detection

When the broadcast_alarms configuration flag is enabled (Alpha: disabled, Testnet: enabled), this evidence is broadcast to the P2P network as a SlashingEvidence message, which the consensus engine processes to execute the slashing penalty without human intervention.

8.6 Optimization Features

· LRU Cache: Recently observed proposals, votes, and block timestamps are stored in a size-limited LRU cache (LruCache), preventing unbounded memory growth.
· Config Validation: Configuration parameters are validated at startup; invalid values produce immediate errors rather than runtime failures.
· Graceful Shutdown: A worker handle tracking system ensures spawned tasks complete within a 5-second timeout before process termination.
· State Persistence: Audit state survives node restarts via save_state() / load_state(), enabling continuous monitoring without data loss.

8.7 Why Sentinel Matters

· No economic barrier: Any community member can audit the chain using existing hardware.
· No trust required: Titans operate with the knowledge that their actions are independently monitored.
· Scalable oversight: Thousands of Sentinels can simultaneously watch 21 Titans, creating a highly redundant audit layer.
· Geographic diversity: Sentinels distributed across jurisdictions independently verify the same chain, eliminating single points of observation failure.
· Structured evidence: Automated slashing proof generation removes the need for manual whistleblowing processes.

