10. Storage Architecture: RocksDB + WAL

10.1 RocksDB Backend

Pacyte uses Facebook/Meta's RocksDB 0.21, an embedded key-value store optimized for solid-state storage with low-latency random access patterns. Five Column Families provide logical data separation:

Column Family Key Format Value Format Purpose
blocks Height (u64 LE, 8 bytes) Block (bincode serialized) Complete block storage
transactions Hash (32 bytes) Transaction (bincode) Transaction lookup by hash
accounts Address (32 bytes) Account (bincode) Account state storage
state_roots Height (u64 LE, 8 bytes) Root hash (32 bytes) Historical state verification
metadata String key Various (u64 LE) Block height, statistics

Configuration:

· Compression: LZ4 (configurable: None, Snappy, LZ4, Zstd)
· Block Cache: 512 MB LRU (configurable)
· Max Open Files: 1000
· Write Options: Configurable sync writes for durability/performance trade-off

10.2 Write-Ahead Log (WAL)

A custom WAL implementation ensures atomicity and durability for all state mutations:

· Write Protocol: All database mutations are written to the WAL before being applied to RocksDB.
· Magic Number: 0x50435741 ("PACW" in ASCII)
· Record Format: Length-prefixed (4-byte LE) with CRC32 checksum
· Entry Types: SaveBlock, SaveTransaction, SaveAccount, DeleteAccount, SaveStateRoot, Batch, Checkpoint
· File Rotation: New WAL file at 64 MB or on checkpoint
· Flush Interval: Configurable, default 100ms

Zero Data Loss Guarantee: On sudden power failure, kernel panic, or unexpected process termination, the WAL is replayed during the next startup to restore the last consistent state. All committed transactions are preserved.

10.3 State Manager

The StateManager provides an account-based state model compatible with Ethereum's execution environment:

· Account Caching: DashMap for concurrent read access with minimal contention.
· Dirty Account Tracking: HashMap of modified accounts pending commit.
· Transfer Execution: Balance verification, nonce validation, dormancy checking, atomic state updates.
· Sparse Merkle Tree (SMT): Real-time state root computation after each block application, enabling efficient light client proofs.
· Block Application: Atomic execution with automatic rollback on failure.
· State Versioning: BTreeMap tracking historical state roots by height.

10.4 Cache Layer

Multi-level caching reduces disk I/O for frequently accessed data:

· BlockCache: LRU cache with hash-to-height index for O(1) lookup by either key.
· TransactionCache: LRU cache keyed by transaction hash.
· AccountCache: LRU cache keyed by account address.
· CacheManager: Unified cache with configurable sizes and optional TTL-based expiration.

10.5 Snapshot Manager

Complete database checkpoint system for backup and synchronization:

· Creates snapshot_{height} directories with blocks.bin and state.bin binary files.
· SnapshotManifest with version, height, block hash, state root, timestamp, and SHA3-256 checksum.
· Supports pruning old snapshots (keep last N configured).
· Manifest integrity verified via cryptographic checksum before restoration.

