11. Peer-to-Peer Network Layer

11.1 P2P Architecture

TCP-based P2P networking built on the Tokio async runtime:

· Listener: Binds to configured address (default 0.0.0.0:9333).
· Connection Handler: Spawns per-peer async task for bidirectional message handling via tokio::select!.
· Bootstrap: Connects to configured bootstrap peers on startup.
· Maintenance Loop: Periodic ping (configurable interval, default 30s), minimum peer count enforcement, ban list cleanup.
· Subscribe: UnboundedReceiver for consuming network messages by consensus engine and Sentinel.

11.2 Peer Manager

Comprehensive peer state tracking:

· State Machine: Connecting → Handshaking → Connected → Disconnected → Banned
· Address-to-ID Mapping: Prevents duplicate connections.
· Ban System: Configurable ban duration with automatic cleanup.
· Peer Statistics: Messages sent/received, bytes transferred, latency tracking, failed pings.
· Random Peer Selection: For gossip fanout and load distribution.

11.3 Message Types

25+ message variants organized by category:

Category Messages Purpose
Handshake Handshake, HandshakeAck Secure peer connection establishment
Keepalive Ping, Pong Connection health monitoring
Peer Discovery GetPeers, Peers, PeerConnected, PeerDisconnected Network topology management
Block Sync GetBlocks, Blocks, NewBlock, GetBlockHeaders, BlockHeaders Chain synchronization
Transactions NewTransaction, GetTransactions, Transactions Transaction propagation
Consensus Proposal, Vote, NewView, Timeout HotStuff BFT protocol messages
State Sync GetStateChunk, StateChunk State synchronization
Error Error Error reporting

11.4 Handshake Protocol

Secure peer connection establishment with genesis hash validation:

1. Initiator sends Handshake with: protocol version, node ID, port, genesis hash, best height, best block hash, capabilities list, timestamp.
2. Responder validates: genesis hash match (critical—prevents cross-chain connections), protocol version compatibility.
3. Responder sends HandshakeAck with: acceptance status, optional peer ID assignment, rejection reason if applicable.

Genesis hash mismatch → immediate rejection, preventing accidental or malicious cross-chain peering at the network level.

11.5 Gossip Protocol

Efficient epidemic message propagation:

· Message deduplication via seen-message LRU cache.
· Configurable fanout to random subset of connected peers.
· Transaction gossip with fee-priority selection.
· Block gossip with full block body forwarding.

